<p>General Motors has been saying <a href="https://www.theverge.com/transportation/804562/gm-apple-carplay-android-auto-gas-cars-mary-barra">for a few years now</a> that it’s going to get rid of CarPlay and Android Auto on its EVs <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2023/04/general-motors-hates-your-iphone/">because, well, reasons</a>.</p>
<p>Well, well, well… maybe not. Ian Wright of CarBuzz <a href="https://carbuzz.com/gm-not-completely-killing-carplay-android-auto/">writes</a>:</p>
<blockquote><p>
  The Cadillac configurator is up for several 2027 models, and we spotted that the <a href="https://carbuzz.com/cars/cadillac/lyriq/2027/">Lyriq</a> still shows CarPlay and Android Auto being standard. The Lyriq is Cadillac’s first all-electric vehicle and a tech-forward vehicle that we think is becoming <a href="https://carbuzz.com/cadillac-lyriq-luxury-that-keeps-getting-better/">one of the best luxury EVs available</a>. So, it piqued our interest that the GM brand was still including the things it was supposed to be phasing out, and we reached out to Cadillac for comment.</p>
<p>  “Cadillac is continuing to evolve its infotainment strategy across the EV portfolio, based on customer feedback, while also prioritizing the native, in-vehicle experience,” a Cadillac representative told CarBuzz. And, the language is telling. The plan was to start the phasing out of phone projection with the 2024 Chevrolet Blazer EV, <a href="https://carbuzz.com/general-motors-combustion-cars-apple-carplay-android-auto/">with CEO Mary Barra saying</a>: “We made a decision to prioritize our EV vehicles during this timeframe, and as we go forward, we’ll continue across the portfolio.”
</p></blockquote>
<p>Wright does a good job at spotting the weasel words in the new Cadillac statement. Three years after a bold pronouncement that GM wasn’t interested in listening to its customers and giving them what they wanted, here comes an “evolve[d] infotainment strategy… based on customer feedback.”</p>
<p>Translation: Oh, people actually do want CarPlay, so we probably should let them have it, or they’re not going to want to buy our cars.</p>
<p>This isn’t how this story needed to go, but if GM wants to silently walk back its stupid pronouncement from 2023, it’s still a win for anyone in the market for a new car.</p>
